title: Indexing architecture
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
status: record
summary: Why queries are set algebra over bitmaps — the complexity wall of conjunctions, one encoding per type, what was deliberately not built — and what became of the resident index.

The design that made filtered, sorted, paginated queries scale (target: about two
million metarecords), from September 2026. Its resident index is gone — the same
evaluator now reads the key-value store's derived key spaces ([[Storage]]) — but the
reasoning holds, and the store follows it.

!! The problem

The data model is entity–attribute–value: about one row per field per metarecord, so
two million metarecords are sixteen million rows. In SQL, every page re-materialised
the whole repository and scanned the field rows for its predicate: a full traversal
was quadratic (×10 data → ×64 time), and even `LIMIT 100` was linear in the repository
(57 ms at 5 000, 421 ms at 50 000).

!! The complexity wall

* ''One indexed dimension plus a limit'' can be sub-linear: `X LIMIT k` is O(log n + k) when an index yields `X` already in order.
* ''A conjunction breaks it.'' No single-attribute index yields `X ∩ Y` in order; intersecting two index-accessible sets costs Ω(min(|X|, |Y|)) in the worst case, and a small result does not imply little work.
* ''A sort independent of the filter'' is the same wall: "this folder, newest first" touches the folder or the date index, whichever is smaller, not the page.
* ''An exact count'' of a large filtered set is O(m) in a B-tree, which stores no subtree cardinalities.

True O(log n + k) for an independent conjunction needs an index on the ''pair'', and one
per pair is a combinatorial explosion. So the aim was not to remove the wall but never
to hit it by accident, to degrade knowingly when a query is intrinsically hard, and to
shrink the constant of the unavoidable O(n) work by three to four orders of magnitude.

!! Set algebra over compressed bitmaps

The established answer (Druid, Pilosa, Oracle's bitmap indexes): each predicate is a
roaring bitmap of dense ids, and `AND`/`OR`/`NOT` are word operations that skip empty
blocks. ''Every field is indexed'', with no declaration — which fields a user filters on
is not knowable in advance, and requiring one would produce "why is this slow?"
surprises. The encoding is chosen from the field's one value type
([[One value type per field name]]): a bitmap per value for categorical fields, reverse
bitmaps (target → sources) for references and trees, and — in the resident design — a
bit-sliced index for numeric and date ranges. The store keeps the first two as value
partitions and postings ([[Sorting and postings]]); a bit-sliced index for numeric
ranges is deferred there.

Counts are ''exact'' and free: the result is a bitmap before it is counted, which is
what took the count wall away. Approximate counts, once proposed, were never needed.

The one planning rule is fixed and needs no statistics: bitmap work first, text scans
last over what survives — "this folder, name matching X" costs the folder (207 → 10 ms).
A page costs the page: fetch or walk by estimated cost ([[Sorting and postings]]).

!! What was deliberately not built

* ''Per-pair composite indexes'' — combinatorial, low value. The one exception considered, a tree-scoped sort index (folder, sort key) for "browse a folder sorted by date", became the store's ordered forest walk instead.
* ''kd-trees / BKD-trees as the general index'' — they need a fixed, low-dimensional space; here the schema is open (hundreds of field names), the data sparse and three-valued (`nothing`, unknown have no coordinate), real queries one-dimensional, conjunctions dynamic, writes continuous (the watcher), and references a graph. A BKD index stays conceivable only as an opt-in secondary for one motif — a correlated numeric box (`date ∈ … AND size ∈ … AND rate ∈ …`) — if measurement ever shows bitmap intersection insufficient there.
* ''The tree as a contiguous id range'' (ids in tree order, a subtree a range) — it would let a large subtree meet a selective predicate without enumerating the subtree, but tree-order ids do not survive moves. The store's descendant bitmaps made a subtree one read anyway ([[The forest in the store]]).

!! What the resident index taught

* A k-way union must be ''one'' union: unioning each child's bitmap into a growing level recopied its containers once per directory, and made an 11 699-file subtree cost 5 ms where the union costs a tenth of that — the same quadratic tail hid in every "union of many value bitmaps".
* Only directories need asking for children: keeping the set of ids that have a child made a level's files — nearly all of it — cost no lookup (5 → 0.6 ms).
* Catching a derived structure up must be paid by the ''writer'', not the next reader: a daemon writing unattended all night handed the first query a full rebuild (10.3 s for `GET /fields` on 50 000 metarecords). In the store, derived data is written in the revision's own transaction ([[Writes and atomicity]]).
* A stored sort key for a path goes stale for a whole subtree at every rename; path keys are rebuilt from the forest instead.
